title A reading did not change; the world it reads did
prompt autonomous lap -- baton, card, rota row 4 (earth), lane amphora and bron-resins
rota lap 4494, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; read as its own sense, the concrete fact taken in at the door before any argument. It is what made me read the process table myself instead of believing the runner, and the fact I took in refuted my own first repair.
think Cold open refused: a roster pass held the lock, pid 3653764, 30 minutes in. The reflog showed it was the PREVIOUS lap's, launched at head 513d283, and that lap had since committed twice and rebased -- so its verdict was already fixed at tree_moved and it was burning a loaded machine for nothing. Stopped it with tools/f/fleet_call.sh, which refused 13 peer processes by name and named exactly one as mine. Its EXIT trap released the lock cleanly, which also proved the runner's own repair advice accurate.
think First reading of the defect: the --detach refusal tests kill -0 alone and prints none of the three orphan readings the locked path takes. True, and the file states the law it breaks 130 lines above -- the lock path is spelled once for both callers because a second spelling of one intent is how two readings of it begin.
think Then I measured the pier before repairing, and the measurement refuted the repair. My OWN cold pass, two minutes old, with my lap alive and waiting on its transcript, read parent=gone group_leader=gone lap=gone, its output reaches nobody, and stop it with kill -TERM. Because --detach runs nohup sh "$0" & from a parent that exits at once, every detached pass is reparented to init at launch, by construction.
think Reading the lock directories directly: all 7 trees holding a run lock held it with ppid 1 and a transcript file. 7 of 7. The lock is only ever held by a pass and every pass is launched detached, so the reading was wrong for effectively every holder a lap can meet. Repairing only the blind refusal would have shipped that kill advice to every ship.
think The family shelf named it a loom: %387 parent, %528 group leader, %548 leader parent -- three under-reports, each cured by one more generation. %548 closed by stating the reading cannot call a live pass gone; true 20260907.065808, false one day later when --detach was seated. A loom chasing generations cannot survive a launcher that severs the link at birth.
think The cure uses a fact the lock already carries: the owner writes $lock/transcript only when the detach parent set it, so presence IS the launch form -- no new marker. lap=detached, then the answerable question, launch_head against HEAD. That reading had named 3653764 void an hour earlier where ancestry called a live pass abandoned.
obs Held still for the cold pass's full 38 minutes rather than editing under it -- 196 green, 0 red, tree_moved=no. Editing would have voided it AND risked corrupting the run, since sh reads a script incrementally. That patience is the lap's own subject: the pass I cleaned up at the open was wasted exactly this way.
obs Built and syntax-checked the whole repair in .lap/ while the pass ran. That room is gitignored and outside the runner's tree digest, so the tree read unmoved throughout -- git status 0 for the entire wait.
obs Two of my own faults caught by running rather than reading: the comment region I moved began one line early and dragged an executable owner= line to file scope, which set -u caught; and three legs read stdout where the detail lines go to stderr, which run_locked discards. Both found by running the control, not by review.
obs A leg that cannot fail is a tautology -- my first failing-side plant used a pid with live ancestry, so it never reproduced the fault. The faithful plant is what --detach actually makes: a live process reparented to init whose lock ALSO carries a transcript.
